CAT = "auwahLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Under the model WHS Act, who holds the primary duty of care for workers' health and safety?",
 'The PCBU', ['The worker', 'The safety regulator', 'The health and safety rep'],
 'The person conducting a business or undertaking (PCBU) has the primary duty to eliminate or minimise risks so far as is reasonably practicable. Workers have duties too, but the primary duty sits with the PCBU.'),
('In WHS law, what does PCBU stand for?',
 'Person conducting a business or undertaking', ['Principal contractor for building undertakings', 'Person controlling a building or unit', 'Permit-holding contractor or builder unit'],
 'PCBU means person conducting a business or undertaking. It covers companies, sole traders, government agencies and principal contractors alike.'),
('Your supervisor gives you a reasonable safety instruction about clipping on. What must you do?',
 'Comply with it as far as you can', ['Comply only if it is written down', 'Comply only if the client asks', 'Check it with the union first'],
 'Workers must comply, so far as they are reasonably able, with any reasonable instruction the PCBU gives so it can meet the WHS Act. The instruction does not need to be in writing.'),
('The PCBU supplies your harness and lanyard. Can you be charged for them?',
 'No, the PCBU must not charge', ['Yes, through a wage deduction', 'Yes, if you take them home', 'Only if you lose them on site'],
 'A PCBU must not charge or levy a worker for anything done or provided for work health and safety, including PPE. The worker must still use and care for it as instructed.'),
('What is the legal status of an approved code of practice?',
 'Practical guidance a court can rely on', ['Optional advice with no legal weight at all', 'A law that overrides the regulations', 'A rule that binds designers only'],
 'Approved codes of practice are admissible in court as evidence of what is known about a hazard and its controls. Following one helps a duty holder show they did what was reasonably practicable.'),
('Construction work with a risk of a person falling more than what height is high risk construction work?',
 '2 metres', ['1 metre', '3 metres', '4 metres'],
 'Under the model WHS Regulations, construction work involving a risk of a person falling more than 2 metres is high risk construction work, so a SWMS is needed before it starts.'),
('What does SWMS stand for?',
 'Safe work method statement', ['Site work management schedule', 'Safety work monitoring sheet', 'Standard work method summary'],
 'A safe work method statement lists the high risk construction work, its hazards and risks, and the controls and how they will be put in place and monitored.'),
('High risk construction work at height is not being done the way its SWMS says. What must happen?',
 'Stop the work until it complies', ['Finish the job, then update it', 'Note it at the next toolbox talk', 'Let the client decide'],
 'If high risk construction work is not carried out in line with its SWMS, the work must stop immediately or as soon as it is safe, and resume only when it follows the SWMS.'),
('A scaffolding high risk work licence is needed to erect a scaffold from which a person or object could fall more than:',
 '4 metres', ['2 metres', '3 metres', '6 metres'],
 'Scaffolds from which a person or object could fall more than 4 metres must be erected, altered and dismantled by a licensed scaffolder or a trainee under their direct supervision.'),
('A boom-type EWP needs a high risk work licence to operate when its boom length is:',
 '11 metres or more', ['6 metres or more', '8 metres or more', '15 metres or more'],
 "Operating a boom-type elevating work platform with a boom of 11 metres or more needs a boom-type EWP high risk work licence, or enrolment in that training under a licence holder's supervision."),
('Before doing any construction work, what training must a worker have completed?',
 'General construction induction', ['A first aid certificate course', 'A basic scaffolding licence course', 'A boom-type EWP operator licence'],
 'A person must not carry out construction work unless they have completed general construction induction training, often called the White Card. Licences are extra and only for licensed tasks.'),
('Who must a PCBU consult when choosing controls for work at height?',
 'The workers who do the work', ['Only the state safety regulator', "Only the building's owner", 'Only the equipment hirer'],
 'A PCBU must consult, so far as is reasonably practicable, with workers who are or are likely to be directly affected. Workers often know where the real fall risks are.'),
('Who can request a review of fall controls they believe are not working?',
 'A health and safety rep', ['Only the building owner', 'Only the equipment supplier', "Only the site's first aider"],
 'Control measures must be reviewed when a health and safety representative requests it and reasonably believes a review trigger applies. Other triggers include a new hazard or a change at the workplace.'),
('When deciding what is reasonably practicable, when is the cost of a control considered?',
 'Last, after the other factors', ['First, before assessing the risk', 'Never, cost is always ignored', 'Only for small businesses'],
 'Cost is weighed last, after the likelihood and severity of harm and the available controls. A control can only be ruled out on cost if the cost is grossly disproportionate to the risk.'),
('Which statement about work health and safety laws across Australia is true?',
 'Details vary by state and territory', ['Every state and territory has identical law', 'Only federal law covers building sites', 'They apply only in New South Wales'],
 'Most jurisdictions have adopted the model WHS laws with local changes, and Victoria has its own OHS laws. Check the rules where you work; licensing and certification details vary by state and territory.'),
('Under the WHS Act, which of these people counts as a worker?',
 'A subcontractor', ['A passer-by', 'A client on a walk-through', 'A neighbour watching'],
 "A worker is anyone who carries out work for a PCBU, including employees, contractors, subcontractors, apprentices, labour hire workers and many volunteers. Visitors are 'other persons' with their own duties."),
('A visitor walks through a site where work at height is under way. What duty do they have?',
 'Take reasonable care for safety', ['None, duties apply to workers only', 'Inspect any scaffold they use', 'Sign the SWMS before entering'],
 'Other persons at a workplace must take reasonable care for their own safety, not adversely affect others, and comply with reasonable instructions so far as they are able.'),
('What duty does an officer, such as a company director, have under WHS laws?',
 'Exercise due diligence', ['Inspect every scaffold', 'Hold a high risk licence', 'Sign every daily permit'],
 'Officers must exercise due diligence to ensure the PCBU complies with its duties, for example by knowing the risks and making sure resources and processes are in place.'),
('A worker falls 3 metres and is admitted to hospital as an in-patient. What must the PCBU do?',
 'Notify the regulator immediately', ['Notify the regulator within 30 days', 'Record it in the site diary only', 'Notify only if the worker dies'],
 'Admission to hospital as an in-patient is a serious injury, which makes it a notifiable incident. The regulator must be told immediately after the PCBU becomes aware of it.'),
('After a notifiable fall from height, what must happen to the incident site?',
 'Leave it undisturbed', ['Clean it up straight away', 'Reopen it after one shift', 'Dismantle the scaffold at once'],
 'The site must not be disturbed until an inspector arrives or directs otherwise. You may still help an injured person, remove a body or make the area safe.'),
('You believe a roof job puts you at serious and immediate risk of falling. What are you entitled to do?',
 'Stop work and tell your supervisor', ['Keep working but work faster', 'Leave the site without telling anyone', 'Finish, then make a complaint'],
 'A worker may cease or refuse unsafe work if they reasonably believe it would expose them to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard. They must tell the PCBU as soon as practicable.'),
('Who must make sure a worker has the training to use a harness system safely?',
 'The PCBU', ['The harness maker', 'The worker alone', 'The regulator'],
 'The PCBU must provide the information, training, instruction and supervision needed to protect workers. Manufacturers supply instructions, but the PCBU makes sure workers are trained.'),
('Which duty holder can best remove a fall risk by placing roof plant at ground level?',
 'The designer', ['The roof plumber', 'The labour hire firm', 'The first aid officer'],
 'Designers of plant and structures must eliminate or minimise risks so far as is reasonably practicable. Designing out the need to work at height is the most effective control.'),
("In a code of practice, what does the word 'must' indicate?",
 'A legal requirement', ['A recommended practice', 'An optional course of action', "The maker's advice"],
 "'Must' means a legal requirement exists that has to be complied with. 'Should' means a recommended course of action and 'may' an optional one."),
]
